{
  "analysis": "Starting fresh. The user can't curl example.com and wants to know why and how to fix it. I need to diagnose the issue with several checks: try curl, check DNS resolution, check connectivity, check proxy/env settings, and system config.",
  "plan": "Run a series of diagnostic commands: attempt curl, check DNS resolution (getent, dig/nslookup), check network connectivity (ping), examine environment proxy variables, and review /etc/resolv.conf and /etc/hosts. This will reveal the root cause.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "curl -v example.com\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "curl fails with 'DNS resolution error'. This points to a DNS/name resolution problem. I need to inspect the resolver configuration and related files.",
  "plan": "Check /etc/resolv.conf for valid nameservers, inspect /etc/hosts, test resolution with getent, and look at nsswitch.conf. These will show whether nameservers are missing/misconfigured or the hosts file is wrong.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /etc/resolv.conf\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /etc/nsswitch.conf 2>/dev/null | grep hosts\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "getent hosts example.com; echo \"exit=$?\"\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /etc/hosts\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Interesting - getent hosts example.com succeeds and returns IPv6 addresses, and nsswitch.conf uses 'files dns'. But curl reports 'DNS resolution error'. The nameserver is 127.0.0.11 (Docker's embedded resolver). The discrepancy suggests curl may be configured to use a proxy, a custom resolver, or only IPv4. I need to inspect environment proxy variables and curl configuration.",
  "plan": "Check proxy environment variables, curl config files (~/.curlrc, /etc/curlrc), and whether curl works with forced IPv4 (-4) or by IP directly. This will isolate whether the problem is a proxy setting, an IPv4-only resolution issue, or a curlrc override.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "env | grep -i proxy\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat ~/.curlrc /etc/curlrc 2>/dev/null; echo '---'\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "curl -4 -v example.com\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "curl --version | head -3\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Key clue: `curl --version` returned 'Could not resolve host: --version' — meaning curl treated '--version' as a hostname. This indicates `curl` is not the real curl binary but a wrapper script/alias that mishandles arguments. System DNS resolution actually works fine (getent returns example.com's addresses). I need to find what curl is.",
  "plan": "Identify the curl command: check for aliases, type, and which. Then inspect the wrapper script to understand why it fails and how to fix (replace with real curl or fix the wrapper).",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "type curl; which curl; alias | grep curl\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "file $(which curl); echo '---'; head -50 $(which curl)\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Root cause found. `/usr/bin/curl` is NOT the real curl — it's a fake wrapper shell script that unconditionally echoes 'DNS resolution error' and exits 11, ignoring all arguments. That's why even `curl --version` failed ('Could not resolve host: --version' — the wrapper discarded the flag). DNS and networking are actually fine (getent resolved example.com). I need to find the real curl binary and restore it.",
  "plan": "Locate the genuine curl binary (likely renamed/moved, or available via the package). Check dpkg for the curl package, search the filesystem for other curl executables, and inspect the wrapper. Then restore real curl by either reinstalling or pointing to the real binary.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "dpkg -L curl 2>/dev/null | grep bin; echo '---'; ls -la /usr/bin/curl*\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la /usr/bin/ | grep -i curl; echo '---'; command -v curl.real curl.bin 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "find / -name 'curl' -type f 2>/dev/null | head; echo '==='; find / -name 'curl*' 2>/dev/null | grep -v /usr/bin/curl | head\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The fake /usr/bin/curl script (46 bytes) is the only curl. The real curl binary appears absent — dpkg -L curl returned no bin entry (curl package likely not properly installed, or the real binary was replaced). The find for other curl files seems to have returned nothing. I need to confirm and restore the real curl. Several options: reinstall via apt, or check if a real binary exists elsewhere. Let me check apt availability and the package state.",
  "plan": "Check whether the curl package is installed and what it provides, check apt-get availability, and look for a backup of the real binary. Likely fix is to reinstall curl with apt (apt-get install --reinstall curl) after removing the fake wrapper, or simply install curl. First confirm network/apt access since DNS works.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "dpkg -l | grep -i curl; echo '---'\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la /bin/curl /usr/local/bin/curl 2>/dev/null; echo '---'; apt-get --version 2>/dev/null | head -1\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Root cause confirmed. `/bin/curl` (same file, /bin is typically a symlink to /usr/bin) is a 46-byte fake wrapper script that ignores all arguments and always prints 'DNS resolution error' then exits 11. DNS resolution and networking are actually healthy — `getent hosts example.com` resolved correctly. The real `curl` binary is not installed at all (no curl package in dpkg). The 'Could not resolve host: --version' earlier was the wrapper dumping our flag as a bogus hostname. The fix is to remove the fake wrapper and install the genuine curl package via apt.",
  "plan": "1) Confirm /bin/curl is the same as /usr/bin/curl. 2) Remove the fake wrapper. 3) Install the real curl with apt-get. 4) Verify curl works against example.com. This restores normal behavior without touching DNS (which is fine).",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -li /bin/curl /usr/bin/curl\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "rm -f /usr/bin/curl /bin/curl\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-get update 2>&1 | tail -5\n",
      "duration": 20.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Confirmed /bin/curl and /usr/bin/curl are the same inode (fake wrapper). I removed it. apt-get update is running; I need to wait for it to finish.",
  "plan": "Wait for apt-get update to complete, then install the real curl package and verify it works.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "",
      "duration": 15.0
    }
  ]
}

